      ],
      "id": "m12vj32"
    },
    {
      "id": "eo3be2h",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Sources of Information about Earth's Interior"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Information about Earth's interior is derived from direct sources (deep mining, volcanic eruptions) and indirect sources (seismic waves, gravity, magnetic field).",
        "P-waves (Primary) travel through solids, liquids, and gases, whereas S-waves (Secondary) travel exclusively through solid media.",
        "The crust consists of lighter continental crust (SIAL - Silica & Aluminium) and denser oceanic crust (SIMA - Silica & Magnesium).",
        "The mantle extends to a depth of 2,900 km, with the weak upper portion called the asthenosphere serving as the primary source of magma.",
        "The Earth's core is divided into a liquid outer core and a solid inner core, primarily composed of heavy elements like nickel and iron (NIFE)."
      ],
      "id": "sgpx2el"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "6igm6cf",
      "content": "Direct Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "85p3eha",
      "content": "• Deep mining operations."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k5dm0yx",
      "content": "• Scientific drilling projects."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ta13l31",
      "content": "• Volcanic eruptions which bring material from inside the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0bni3tj",
      "content": "Indirect Sources"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ewnnl9y",
      "content": "• Seismic waves generated by earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tdx0qcm",
      "content": "• Study of meteorites."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "35qy05c",
      "content": "• Gravitational and magnetic field studies."
    },
    {
      "id": "v1rnw75",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Seismic Waves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ava7uuk",
      "content": "• Seismic waves are energy waves generated during earthquakes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5p1yh7x",
      "content": "• They help scientists understand the structure of Earth's interior."
    },
    {
      "id": "44pl2h8",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Wave Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Nature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Characteristics</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>P Waves (Primary)</p>",
            "<p>Compressional (body waves)</p>",
            "<p>Travel through solids, liquids and gases; fastest</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>S Waves (Secondary)</p>",
            "<p>Transverse (body waves)</p>",
            "<p>Travel only through solids</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>R Waves (Surface)</p>",
            "<p>Surface waves</p>",
            "<p>Large amplitude and greater destruction</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>L Waves (Surface)</p>",
            "<p>Surface waves</p>",
            "<p>Cause maximum destruction during earthquakes</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Seismic waves are the most important indirect source for understanding the internal structure and composition of the Earth.",
      "id": "tf9yhba"
    },
    {
      "id": "bcp0vp3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Layers of the Earth"
    },
